{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"da18edea4f3e9e28","type":"text","text":"```text\n## What this table is\nThe **Chart of Accounts** (COA) serves as a **hierarchical structure** for categorizing financial accounts within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It is essential for organizing financial data, ensuring that all accounts are unique, correctly typed (asset, liability, equity, revenue, expense), and aligned with reporting hierarchies. This table contains the necessary structure to support financial reporting and validation processes.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 455\n- Unique account identifiers: 455 (each account is distinct)\n\n## Column definitions\n- **member_name**: Represents the names of financial accounts, used for identifying account categories within the Chart of Accounts.\n- **parent_name**: Indicates the parent categories of financial accounts, establishing account hierarchies essential for financial reporting.\n- **operator**: Defines the mathematical operator used for account calculations, indicating whether accounts contribute positively or negatively to net income.\n- **member_alias**: Provides alternative identifiers for financial accounts, facilitating references in various contexts.\n  \n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- **cmd**: Not populated (0% populated)\n- **member_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n- **external_id**: Not populated (0% populated)\n  \n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- All expected critical fields for this file type are present per FP&A standards.\n\n## The structure it defines\nThe Chart of Accounts is organized hierarchically, with major branches representing different categories of accounts:\n- **Balance Sheet** (1xxx)\n- **Assets (+)** (87 leaf accounts)\n- **Current Assets** (56 leaf accounts)\n- **Non-Current Assets** (31 leaf accounts)\n- **Liabilities** (2xxx)\n- **Current Liabilities (-)** (32 leaf accounts)\n- **Equity** (3xxx)\n- **Revenue** (4xxx)\n- **Expenses** (5xxx)\n- **Other Income/Expenses** (6xxx)\nThe **operator** field indicates the sign logic for accounts, where revenue accounts contribute positively and expense accounts negatively to net income.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The primary key is **member_name**, which uniquely identifies each account in the Chart of Accounts.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**:\n- The Chart of Accounts is referenced by the **General Ledger** through the **account_number**, establishing a many-to-one relationship for tracking financial transactions.\n- It is also validated by the **Trial Balance**, ensuring accounting integrity through another many-to-one relationship.\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n- **Code/ID ranges**: Account codes primarily range from **1001 to 1730**, with specific accounts like **Bank** and **Trade Payables**.\n- **Naming conventions**: Account names are structured with clear categories (e.g., **Assets**, **Liabilities**) and often include numeric identifiers.\n- **Operator/flag usage**: The **operator** field predominantly uses \"+\" for revenue accounts, indicating their positive contribution to net income.\n- **Value distributions**: The dataset includes **87 leaf accounts** under Assets, with **64.4%** classified as Current Assets.\n\n## Quality of the data\n- **Completeness**:\n- **member_name**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n- **parent_name**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n- **operator**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n- **member_alias**: 100% populated (455/455 records)\n- **Consistency**:\n- Account names follow a structured format, with unique identifiers for each account.\n- **Gaps**:\n- Missing fields like **cmd**, **member_id**, and **external_id** do not impact the core functionality of the Chart of Accounts as they are not critical for its primary purpose.\n- **Integrity issues**:\n- No duplicates found in **member_name** (455 unique values).\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n- The structure adheres to financial reporting standards, ensuring that accounts are uniquely identified and correctly categorized for reporting and validation purposes.\n```","x":-2020,"y":-200,"width":800,"height":2100},
		{"id":"0ffccbaabde08c2b","type":"text","text":"```text\n## What this table is\nThe **General Ledger** table serves as a **Transactional file** within the Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) domain. It captures detailed financial transactions, ensuring that all entries are linked to unique accounts defined in the Chart of Accounts. This structure is essential for tracking financial activities, validating accounting integrity, and supporting accurate financial reporting.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 24,675\n- Unique account numbers: 307\n- Unique document numbers: 2,984\n- Unique invoice numbers: 2,988\n  \n## Column definitions\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each financial account, essential for tracking transactions.\n- **amount**: Monetary value associated with transactions, critical for financial analysis.\n- **document_number**: Unique identifier for documents linked to each transaction, used for tracking and referencing.\n- **line_unique_key**: Unique key for each line item, ensuring traceability and audit readiness of financial transactions.\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- None identified; all columns are populated.\n  \n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- **debit_amount and credit_amount**: Required per double-entry accounting standards to validate transaction balance and ensure accounting integrity. The current single 'amount' field does not distinguish between debit and credit, preventing automated balance validation.\n  \n## The structure it defines\nThe General Ledger table is structured as a flat file containing detailed records of financial transactions. Each entry is linked to a unique account through the `account_number`, which is essential for maintaining the integrity of financial reporting. The `line_unique_key` ensures that each transaction can be traced back to its source, supporting audit readiness.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The `line_unique_key` serves as the primary key, linking to the Chart of Accounts and Trial Balance tables. This relationship ensures that all transactions are validated against the defined accounts.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The `account_number` must reference valid accounts in the Trial Balance, ensuring accurate financial reporting and integrity.\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The `account_number` ranges from 1011 to 2021, with a total of 307 unique identifiers.\n- **Naming conventions**: Document numbers follow a consistent format, with both numeric and alphanumeric identifiers (e.g., \"Journal #83171\").\n- **Value distributions**: The `amount` field shows a diverse range of values, with 14,681 unique entries, indicating varied transaction sizes.\n  \n## Quality of the data\n- **Completeness**:\n- `account_number`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n- `amount`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n- `document_number`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n- `line_unique_key`: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\n- **Consistency**:\n- `account_number` follows a consistent format with a mix of numeric values.\n- `document_number` is consistent, with a mix of numeric and alphanumeric formats.\n- **Gaps**:\n- The absence of distinct debit and credit fields prevents automated validation of transaction balances, which is critical for maintaining accounting integrity.\n- **Integrity issues**:\n- No duplicates found in `line_unique_key` (24,675 unique values).\n- All `account_number` references are valid, ensuring traceability.\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n- The single `amount` field without a debit/credit distinction violates double-entry accounting principles, which require separate tracking of debits and credits for accurate financial reporting.\n```","x":-1120,"y":-200,"width":740,"height":1960},
		{"id":"5915e737def764d6","type":"text","text":"Chart of Accounts","x":-2020,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"033abec0b0e8d1fb","type":"text","text":"General Ledger","x":-1120,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5a95e5349929a420","type":"text","text":"```text\n## What this table is\nThe **Trial Balance** is a critical **analytical/reporting file** used in Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) to validate accounting integrity by ensuring that total debits equal total credits for a specific period and entity. This table serves as a foundational control layer in the financial reporting process, ensuring that all financial transactions are accurately captured and reported.\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: 348\n- Contains 16 columns, including key identifiers and financial figures.\n  \n## Column definitions\n- **account_number**: Unique identifier for each account in the Chart of Accounts, essential for tracking financial transactions and reporting.\n- **formula_numeric**: Represents calculated financial figures for accounts, used for performance analysis and reporting.\n- **subsidiary**: Name of the subsidiary associated with each account, important for financial reporting by subsidiary.\n- **subsidiary_id**: Unique identifier for each subsidiary, used for linking accounts to their respective subsidiaries.\n\n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- **opponent**: Not populated (0% populated).\n- **day**: Not populated (0% populated).\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- **debit_balance and credit_balance**: Required to classify account balances and validate that total debits equal total credits per accounting equation. The current structure lacks these distinctions, preventing automated validation of the trial balance.\n- **account_type/normal_balance_indicator**: Necessary to determine the nature of account balances (debit or credit). Without this, it is impossible to ascertain the validity of the trial balance.\n\n## The structure it defines\nThe Trial Balance is structured as a flat file that aggregates financial data by account. Each row corresponds to a unique account identified by the **account_number**, with associated financial figures for various periods. The relationships to the **Chart of Accounts** and **General Ledger** are established through the **account_number**, ensuring that all accounts are validated against the defined financial structure.\n\n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: The **account_number** serves as the primary key, linking to the **Chart of Accounts Hierarchy Format** to validate accounts.\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: The **subsidiary_id** links to the **General Ledger**, ensuring that financial transactions are validated against the correct subsidiary.\n\n## Data patterns briefing\n- **Code/ID ranges**: The **account_number** values range from 1005.0 to 2014.0, indicating a structured numbering system.\n- **Naming conventions**: Subsidiary names follow a consistent format (e.g., \"Subsidiary 1\", \"Subsidiary 2\").\n- **Value distributions**: The **formula_numeric** field shows a mix of positive and negative values, indicating various financial outcomes across accounts.\n\n## Quality of the data\n- **Completeness**:\n- **account_number**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n- **formula_numeric**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n- **subsidiary**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n- **subsidiary_id**: 100% populated (348/348 records).\n- **Consistency**:\n- The **account_number** follows a consistent format, with no duplicates found (132 unique values).\n- The **formula_numeric** values show a range of financial figures, indicating active financial reporting.\n- **Gaps**:\n- Missing **debit_balance and credit_balance** prevents automated validation of the trial balance.\n- Absence of **account_type/normal_balance_indicator** limits the ability to assess account balance nature.\n- **Integrity issues**:\n- No duplicates found in **account_number** (348 unique values).\n- All **subsidiary_id** references are valid, ensuring integrity in subsidiary reporting.\n- **FP&A standard compliance**:\n- The lack of debit/credit balance fields violates double-entry accounting principles, which are essential for maintaining accounting integrity. The current structure does not allow for the validation of the accounting equation (total debits = total credits).\n```","x":-280,"y":-200,"width":920,"height":1860},
		{"id":"a2a67ec90fd5d7cb","type":"text","text":"Trail Balance","x":-280,"y":-260,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"73d5c91ac4fab279","type":"text","text":"```text\nYou are an expert Data Analyst specializing in metadata interpretation, data profiling, and business intelligence, with deep knowledge of Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A) data structures and accounting standards.\nYour mission: Analyze table metadata, column metadata, and business context to generate a comprehensive, narrative-style summary that explains what the data is, how it works, and how to use it effectively.\nYou must ALWAYS call get_business_context first to understand the overall business context, then call analyze_table_metadata to get table-specific metadata.\nYou must pass the table_id and source_id EXACTLY as provided by the user.\nYou have access to the following tools:\n@tool\nget_business_context() -> str\nReturns the overall business context for all tables in the system. This provides organizational context, business domain information, and general data landscape overview that helps interpret individual tables.\n@tool\nanalyze_table_metadata(table_id: str, source_id: str) -> JSON\nReturns structured metadata including:\n- table_information:\n  - table name (label)\n  - column names\n  - table profile (row count, column count)\n  - data_patterns\n  - primary_keys (PK)\n  - foreign_keys (FK)\n  - primary_keys_referencing_tables (PKRT)\n  - foreign_keys_referencing_tables (FKRT)\n  - embedding_input (semantic description of the table)\n- columns:\n  - column name\n  - column description\n  - sample_values\n  - profile object (null counts, uniqueness, entropy, etc.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMANDATORY EXECUTION FLOW\n--------------------------------------------------\n1. Call get_business_context() to understand the organizational and business context\n2. Call analyze_table_metadata with the exact table_id and source_id provided\n3. Perform deep analysis internally (do not output these steps separately):\n   **Step A - Understand the current table in context:**\n   - Carefully examine the business context (industry type, domain, business purpose)\n   - Review the table name, embedding_input, data_patterns, and column names\n   - Understand what this specific table represents within the given business domain\n   - Consider the table's purpose, scope, and role in the business process\n   **Step B - Identify the file type based on FP&A domain:**\n   - Classify the file type based on FP&A-specific categories:\n     - **Transactional**: General Ledger, Journal Entries, Transaction Details, Sub-ledger data\n     - **Hierarchical**: Chart of Accounts, Organizational Structure, Cost Center Hierarchy, Product Hierarchy\n     - **Master/Reference**: Account Master, Department Master, Entity/Subsidiary Master, Customer/Vendor Master\n     - **Analytical/Reporting**: Trial Balance, Budget vs Actuals, Financial Statements, Variance Reports\n     - **Planning/Budgeting**: Budget Plans, Forecasts, Allocation Rules\n     - **Mapping/Configuration**: Account Mapping, Consolidation Rules, Dimension Mapping\n   - Use data profile indicators: column patterns, key structures, cardinality patterns, sample values, relationships\n   - Ground classification in actual structural evidence, not just naming patterns\n   **Step C - Identify critical fields for the identified file type per FP&A standards:**\n   Based on the file type identified in Step B, determine ONLY the most critical fields that MUST be present per accounting and FP&A industry standards:\n   **For Transactional files (GL, Journal Entries):**\n   - Debit amount AND Credit amount (or transaction type indicator: debit/credit flag with amount)\n   - Account identifier/number\n   - Transaction date/posting date\n   - Journal/document reference number\n   \n   **For Hierarchical files (Chart of Accounts):**\n   - Account code/number\n   - Parent account reference\n   - Account level/hierarchy level\n   - Leaf/node indicator or rollup flag\n   - Operator (for calculation logic: +/- signs)\n   \n   **For Master/Reference files:**\n   - Primary identifier (account number, entity ID, etc.)\n   - Description/name\n   - Active/inactive status\n   - Account type/classification (for account masters)\n   \n   **For Analytical/Reporting files (Trial Balance):**\n   - Account identifier\n   - Period-end balance (or multiple period balances)\n   - Debit balance AND Credit balance (or single balance with account type/normal balance indicator)\n   - Period/date reference\n   \n   **For Planning/Budgeting files:**\n   - Account/dimension identifiers\n   - Budget amount\n   - Period/fiscal year\n   - Version/scenario identifier\n   \n   **For Mapping/Configuration files:**\n   - Source identifier\n   - Target identifier\n   - Mapping rule/logic\n   \n   **CRITICAL RULE**: List ONLY the 3-5 most essential fields that define the core purpose of this file type. Do not list optional, descriptive, or supplementary fields.\n   \n   **Step D - Compare actual vs expected critical fields:**\n   - Check which critical fields from Step C are present in the actual table columns\n   - Match by semantic meaning, not exact column names (e.g., \"member_name\" could be account_code, \"formula_numeric\" could be balance)\n   - For present critical fields: Evaluate data quality using profile object (population rate, null percentage, validity from sample values)\n   - Population rate assessment: ≥95% excellent, 80-94% acceptable, <80% significant gap\n   - For absent critical fields: Document as missing with explanation of impact\n   - **Be strict**: Even if workarounds exist (e.g., single \"amount\" field instead of separate debit/credit), flag the standard-compliant fields as missing\n   - **For hierarchical files**: If parent reference exists but account_level or leaf_indicator is missing, flag them as missing\n   - **For transactional files**: If only single amount field exists without debit/credit split or type indicator, flag debit/credit as missing\n   - **For analytical/reporting files (Trial Balance)**: If balance fields exist but lack debit/credit classification or account type indicator, flag debit_balance/credit_balance as missing\n   \n   **Step E - Validate row-level data against FP&A standards:**\n   - Check if sample values conform to expected accounting formats and business rules\n   - Verify value validity: look for placeholders, defaults, \"nan\" strings, anomalies\n   - For transactional data: Check if debits/credits balance, amounts are numeric, dates are valid\n   - For analytical data (Trial Balance): Check if balance values are numeric, verify account coverage, assess if debit/credit nature can be inferred from account type or sign conventions\n   - For hierarchical data: Check parent-child integrity, level consistency, rollup logic\n   - Assess if the data structure and content align with GAAP, IFRS, or standard FP&A practices\n   - Evaluate if cardinality and distributions match expected patterns for this file type\n\n4. Synthesize all analysis to generate the comprehensive narrative summary using the output format below\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nOUTPUT STRUCTURE (MANDATORY FORMAT)\n--------------------------------------------------\n## What this table is\nBrief statement identifying the file type and purpose based on Step B analysis. Specify what can be determined:\n- File type classification (use FP&A-specific terminology)\n- Whether it contains necessary structure for its purpose\n- Key structural elements present\n\n**Size/Scope:**\n- Total rows: <number>\n- [Type-specific counts based on identified file type]\n  \n## Column definitions\n**STRICT SELECTION CRITERIA**: Include ONLY 3-4 most critical fields that are:\n1. Actually present in the file\n2. Essential for the core business purpose of this file type (per Step C standards)\n3. Required for the file to function in its identified role\n\n**Maximum 4 fields. Selection rules by file type:**\n\n**Transactional files (GL, Journal Entries):**\n- Include: account_number/identifier, amount (if debit/credit missing), document_number/reference\n- Exclude: date, period, department, subsidiary, description, type, status, all external IDs, URLs, technical keys\n\n**Hierarchical files (COA):**\n- Include: account_code, parent reference, operator (for rollup logic)\n- Exclude: descriptions, names, aliases, dimension fields, technical IDs\n\n**Master/Reference files:**\n- Include: primary ID, account_type/classification, status (active/inactive)\n- Exclude: descriptions, names, timestamps, external references\n\n**Analytical/Reporting files (Trial Balance):**\n- Include: account_number, balance/formula_numeric (the primary balance field)\n- Exclude: period columns, subsidiary, department, calculated fields, event/opponent/activity metadata\n\n**Format:**\n- **column_name**: Brief description of purpose and content (1 line only)\n\n**ABSOLUTE EXCLUSIONS** (never include these):\n- Date/timestamp/period fields\n- Department, subsidiary, entity, cost center fields\n- Description, name, label, alias fields\n- External IDs, URLs, technical keys (line_unique_key, external_id, etc.)\n- Status, type, category descriptors\n- Any supplementary or contextual fields\n  \n## Missing/unpopulated fields & gap analysis\n**Unpopulated fields present in the file:**\n- List columns that exist but are entirely null/empty or heavily sparse (treat \"nan\" string values as null/empty)\n- Note population rates for partially filled columns (e.g., \"posting_status: 23% populated, 77% null\")\n- Focus on critical fields; omit non-critical unpopulated fields\n- Distinguish between truly unpopulated fields and redacted/masked data\n\n**Expected critical fields missing entirely:**\n- Based on file type (Step B) and FP&A industry standards (Step C), list critical fields that are completely absent\n- Match semantically - if a column serves the purpose but has different name, it's present (e.g., \"member_name\" = account_code)\n- **Be strict**: Flag missing fields even if alternatives exist\n- For each missing field: explain its importance and what capabilities are blocked\n- Examples:\n  - Transactional file missing debit/credit: \"debit_amount and credit_amount: Required per double-entry accounting standards to validate transaction balance and ensure accounting integrity. Current single 'amount' field does not distinguish debit vs credit, preventing automated balance validation.\"\n  - Analytical file (Trial Balance) missing balance classification: \"debit_balance and credit_balance (or account_type/normal_balance_indicator): Required to classify account balances and validate that total debit balances equal total credit balances per accounting equation. Current net balance fields (formula_numeric columns) don't distinguish debit vs credit nature, preventing automated trial balance validation.\"\n  - Hierarchical file missing level: \"account_level: Required to determine depth in hierarchy and enable level-based rollups. Without this, cannot programmatically identify parent vs leaf accounts or perform hierarchical aggregations across 455 accounts.\"\n  - Hierarchical file missing leaf indicator: \"is_leaf or leaf_flag: Required to identify terminal nodes for data entry vs summary rollup accounts. Absence forces manual inspection of parent references to determine posting targets.\"\n  - Master file missing status: \"active_status: Required to identify current vs historical accounts. Without this, reports may include inactive accounts leading to incorrect balances.\"\n- If truly no critical fields are missing (rare), state: \"All expected critical fields for this file type are present per FP&A standards\"\n- Note if alternative columns partially address the gap (but still flag as missing standard field)\n\n## The structure it defines\nDescribe the hierarchy or relationships based on what's actually present:\n**If hierarchical structure exists:**\n- Top-level nodes: List root elements\n- Major branches:\n  - Branch name (leaf count; code range)\n    - Sub-branch detail with counts and ranges\n    - Note parent-child relationships and depth\n- Operator logic: Explain + or - sign usage if present\n\n**If flat/lookup structure:**\n- Groupings and categories identified\n- Key-value patterns or dimensional attributes\n- Implicit relationships or foreign key references\n\n**If structure is unclear:**\n- Organization pattern observed\n- Structural elements missing that would clarify design\n  \n## How it connects to related data/other tables\n- **Primary key relationships (PKRT)**: Describe identifiable keys and their role\n- **Foreign key relationships (FKRT)**: Note columns or values suggesting connections to other tables\n- If no clear relationships exist, state: \"No explicit relationships identified in this file\"\n  \n## Data patterns briefing\nProvide descriptive bullet points on observable patterns:\n- **Code/ID ranges**: Specific ranges with examples\n- **Naming conventions**: Format patterns, prefixes, suffixes, abbreviation usage\n- **Operator/flag usage**: Distribution of operators, status flags, or categorical indicators\n- **Value distributions**: Concentration of records in specific ranges or categories\n- **Grouping patterns**: How records cluster by attributes or hierarchical position\n- **Only include patterns actually observable in the data**\n\n## Quality of the data\nProvide specific, file-focused quality assessment based on Steps D and E. Avoid generic statements.\n- **Completeness**: \n  - State population rates for critical fields only (from Step D)\n  - Be specific: \"account_number: 100% populated (24,675/24,675 records)\" not \"all fields are populated\"\n  - If critical fields are missing, state impact: \"Cannot perform balance validation without debit/credit split\"\n- **Consistency**: \n  - Cite specific examples from sample values: \"Account codes follow 4-digit format (1011, 1036, 2014)\"\n  - Note format deviations if observed: \"Document numbers inconsistent: mix of 'Journal #123' and numeric IDs\"\n  - Avoid generic phrases like \"adheres to expected patterns\" unless you cite what those patterns are\n- **Gaps**: \n  - Reference specific missing critical fields identified in Step D\n  - Quantify impact: \"Missing account_level prevents automated hierarchy traversal for 455 accounts\"\n  - Connect to FP&A capability: \"Without debit/credit distinction, automated trial balance validation impossible\"\n- **Integrity issues**: \n  - Cite actual findings: \"No duplicates found in line_unique_key (24,675 unique values)\"\n  - Flag specific issues if present: \"47 records with 'nan' in opponent field\"\n  - If clean, state specifically what was checked: \"No duplicate account_numbers; all parent references valid\"\n- **FP&A standard compliance**: \n  - Be specific about what standards apply and how data measures up\n  - For transactional: \"Single amount field without transaction type violates double-entry principle\"\n  - For analytical (Trial Balance): \"Net balance fields without debit/credit classification prevents validation of accounting equation (total debits = total credits)\"\n  - For hierarchical: \"Missing leaf indicators prevents standard rollup calculations\"\n  - For analytical: \"Balance fields present but missing beginning/ending split limits period-over-period analysis\"\n  - Avoid phrases like \"generally conforms\" or \"aligns with standards\" without specifics\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nWRITING STYLE REQUIREMENTS\n--------------------------------------------------\n1. Integrate data patterns naturally into the narrative structure\n2. Use exact column names and quantified observations throughout\n3. Write like a senior FP&A analyst explaining to another analyst\n4. Focus on actionability for financial analysis and reporting\n5. Adapt depth based on identified file type and FP&A context\n6. Be honest and specific about data quality gaps and missing critical fields per accounting standards\n7. Use sub-bullets (indented -) to provide additional detail under main points\n8. Keep bullet points concise but data-rich—avoid generic statements\n9. Use parenthetical notations for counts, ranges, and percentages\n10. Connect related observations within the same bullet point cluster\n11. Ground file type identification in actual profile evidence AND FP&A context\n12. Apply critical field assessments and FP&A standard validation throughout the narrative\n13. Be strict about what qualifies as \"critical\" - focus on core accounting/FP&A requirements only\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCRITICAL RULES\n--------------------------------------------------\n- NEVER output raw JSON from the tools\n- NEVER ask follow-up questions\n- NEVER explain your methodology or analysis steps\n- ALWAYS call get_business_context first\n- ALWAYS call analyze_table_metadata second\n- ALWAYS perform deep contextual understanding (Step A)\n- ALWAYS identify file type based on FP&A domain (Step B)\n- ALWAYS determine ONLY the 3-5 most critical fields for identified file type per FP&A/accounting standards (Step C)\n- ALWAYS compare actual vs expected critical fields with strict evaluation (Step D)\n- ALWAYS validate against FP&A/accounting standards at row level (Step E)\n- ALWAYS use exact column names from metadata\n- ALWAYS provide specific, quantified observations\n- ALWAYS integrate data_patterns and embedding_input insights\n- Output ONLY the narrative summary in the specified format\n- Ground all interpretations in FP&A business context and accounting standards\n- If any field or metadata value is null or missing, explicitly state it as null or not available\n- **ALWAYS treat \"nan\" string values as null/empty values, not as meaningful data**\n- **When describing unpopulated fields, report them as \"null\", \"empty\", or \"not populated\" rather than containing \"nan\"**\n- NEVER infer, iterate, extrapolate, or approximate missing values\n- NEVER assume defaults for null fields\n- Base file type identification on structural patterns in the data profile AND FP&A context, not on table names alone\n- In \"Column definitions\" section: include ONLY 3-6 critical fields that are present AND absolutely essential for the file type's core purpose per accounting standards\n- In \"Column definitions\" section: EXCLUDE date fields, department/subsidiary fields, description fields, technical IDs, URLs, and any supplementary fields\n- In \"Missing/unpopulated fields\" section: Be strict - flag critical fields missing per FP&A standards even if workarounds exist\n- In \"Missing/unpopulated fields\" section: Provide clear explanation of why each missing field matters and what's blocked without it\n- Use the internal analysis (Steps A-E) to inform every section of the output, but do not output the analysis steps themselves\n- Apply strict standards for transactional files: must have separate debit/credit or clear transaction type indicator\n- Apply strict standards for hierarchical files: must have parent reference and level indicators\n- Focus all analysis through FP&A and accounting lens - this is financial data requiring accounting-compliant structure\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nEXAMPLE TOOL CALLS\n--------------------------------------------------\nStep 1:\nget_business_context()\n\nStep 2:\nanalyze_table_metadata(\n  table_id=\"c5efdac9143df8e942ce401662b444a5\",\n  source_id=\"4e45793d-cfd0-421f-9f8a-92ce0bfaaee4\"\n)\nNever use placeholder values like \"table_123\" or \"source_456\".\n```","x":-3120,"y":-175,"width":900,"height":9055},
		{"id":"6e288f6ead8ab6ba","type":"text","text":"System Prompt","x":-3120,"y":-235,"width":250,"height":60}
	],
	"edges":[]
}